          {
            "id": "p9-q8-short-method",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Square the sum of the side lengths and subtract the sum of their squares to find twice the area.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Let the side lengths be $x\\text{ m}$ and $y\\text{ m}$. From the perimeter,\n$$\\begin{align*}2x+2y&=16\\\\x+y&=8\\end{align*}$$\nBy Pythagoras, $x^2+y^2=36$. Therefore,\n$$\\begin{align*}(x+y)^2&=64\\\\x^2+2xy+y^2&=64\\\\2xy+(x^2+y^2)&=64\\\\2xy+36&=64\\\\2xy&=28\\\\xy&=14\\end{align*}$$\nThe area is $xy=14\\text{ m}^2$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 92
  },
